{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# S2 — honour `Retry-After` on 429, scoped to IDEMPOTENT READS\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nSplit out of [rebar:b586-2fda-8a15-4260] (ten BLOCK rounds; full record on that ticket). S2 is the\nPOLICY half; [rebar:f2f3-9cb1-335b-4e31] (S1) was the mechanical relocation that made room for it.\n**S1 HAS LANDED** — Gerrit 1179, on `main` as commit `529e802da0`. `transport.py` is now 683 lines\n(117 of headroom under the locked 800 cap) and the retry cluster lives in\n`adapters/jira_datacenter/retry.py`. **Every line citation in this plan was re-probed against that\npost-S1 tree**; the previous revision's citations were taken pre-S1 and were all stale, which is\nwhat a round of review correctly caught.\n\nJira Data Center has built-in rate limiting (8.6+, **DC only**, **OFF BY DEFAULT** — an admin\nenables it), a per-user token bucket. rebar's DC transport does not handle it:\n`_with_connection_retry` retries only connection-level faults and its docstring states that\n`jira.exceptions.JIRAError` — any HTTP 4xx/5xx — is NOT in the retry set, so a 429 propagates as a\nhard failure on the first occurrence, on an instance that was merely asking rebar to slow down.\n\nTwo constraints shape the whole fix:\n1. **A static pacing policy is NOT implementable.** The limit is admin-configured and may be absent\n   entirely (pre-8.6, Server, or simply disabled), so honour headers WHEN PRESENT and degrade\n   gracefully when absent.\n2. **Atlassian prescribes `Retry-After` PLUS up to 20% jitter**, which differs from ADR-0036's\n   backoff curve (designed for transient connectivity, where the server offers no guidance).\n   Jitter is not decoration: every rebar instance hitting the same bucket would otherwise retry in\n   lockstep.\n\n**VERIFY THE NUMBERS BEFORE BUILDING TO THEM** — \"off by default\", \"8.6+\" and \"retry-after + 20%\njitter\" come from OSS research with Atlassian citations, not from testing, and that research\nretracted two of its other claims on a second pass.\n\n## Approach\n\n**Reuse what exists — do not build a third `Retry-After` implementation.**\n`_errors.parse_retry_after()` and `_errors.MAX_BACKOFF_S` already exist, `dispatch_one.py:41`\nalready imports both, and `dispatch_one.py:148-172` is ALREADY a 429-honouring retry path for the\nREST transport. Read that path first and mirror it; a second parser is the finding that opened\nround 1.\n\n**MIRROR IT, BUT NOT EXACTLY — one deliberate divergence, stated so it is not read as a slip.**\nThat existing path applies `min(MAX_BACKOFF_S, retry_after)` with **no jitter** on the\n`Retry-After` branch (it jitters only the ADR-0036 curve, `dispatch_one.py:167-169`). Atlassian\nprescribes `Retry-After` PLUS up to 20% jitter for the DC token bucket, so this story's 429 branch\njitters where the existing one does not. Reuse the PARSER and the CONSTANT; do not copy the delay\narithmetic verbatim.\n\n**SCOPE THE RETRY TO IDEMPOTENT READS. This is the load-bearing constraint.**\n`_with_connection_retry` is the single choke point for ALL 15 transport call sites INCLUDING\n`create_issue`, `add_comment` and `add_label`, and its docstring already records why JIRAError is\nunretried: \"retrying a mutation on an HTTP error risks duplicates\". Retrying 429s there would\n**reintroduce the duplicate-issue class [rebar:21fc-51d7-90ca-4a03] just fixed** — a 429 can arrive\nafter the server began the write, and nothing in the response distinguishes that from rejection at\nthe gate. So: per-CALL opt-in, **defaulting to OFF**, so a mutation added later cannot inherit the\nretry by omission.\n\n**THE OPT-IN HAS TO REACH THE RETRY LAYER THROUGH TWO DISTINCT PATHS, NOT ONE.** A previous\nrevision threaded the flag through `_call_logged` alone and was blocked for it; the correction is\nrecorded here because the gap is invisible unless the call sites are read individually:\n\n  * PATH A — `_paged_search` -> `_call_logged` -> `_with_connection_retry`. `_paged_search`\n    (`transport.py:315`) issues its search through `_call_logged` (`transport.py:165`), which today\n    calls `_with_connection_retry(fn)` with **no keyword argument at all**. So `_call_logged` is an\n    EDIT TARGET, not merely a conduit: it must accept `rate_limit_retry` and forward it. Omitting\n    it makes the flag a no-op that still type-checks.\n  * PATH B — a DIRECT `_with_connection_retry` call that bypasses `_call_logged` entirely.\n    `get_comment_map` fetches comments **one request per issue** at `transport.py:367`\n    (`_with_connection_retry(lambda k=key: self._client.comments(k))`). That is the\n    HIGHEST-VOLUME read in a pass and therefore the single most likely thing to trip a token\n    bucket — and it is precisely the call PATH A does not reach. It opts in directly.\n\nGetting only PATH A would ship a rate-limit fix that misses the read most likely to be rate\nlimited, while every test written against `_paged_search` passed.\n\n**WHAT ESCAPES WHEN THE CAP IS EXHAUSTED.** `BackendHTTPError` with `code == 429`, never the raw\n`JIRAError`. This is not a preference: it is what the boundary already does.\n`_with_connection_retry` (now `retry.py:121`) catches the library error types and raises\n`_as_backend_http_error(exc) from exc`, which reads `.status_code` into `BackendHTTPError.code`\nusing urllib's spelling SO THAT the core's existing `except urllib.error.HTTPError` branches\nclassify a DC failure exactly as they classify a Cloud one. Leaking `JIRAError` past that boundary\nis the actual regression risk here, and J10 pinned the neutral exception contract precisely to stop\nit. (A library error with no usable status degrades to `code=0`, which no core branch mistakes for\na 404 or a success.)\n\n## Scope\n\nFour files' worth of edits, and the opted-in read call sites are ENUMERATED HERE — this is the\nsingle enumeration in the document, and Testing and the ACs refer back to it rather than restating\nit. (The previous revision had Scope defer to Testing while Testing deferred to Scope, so neither\nlisted anything; that circularity was a blocking finding.)\n\n- `adapters/jira_datacenter/retry.py` — `_with_connection_retry` gains\n  `rate_limit_retry: bool = False`; the 429 branch, `Retry-After` parsing, jitter-then-clamp.\n- `adapters/jira_datacenter/transport.py`:\n  - `_call_logged` (`:165`) — gains `rate_limit_retry: bool = False` and FORWARDS it. Without this\n    edit the flag cannot reach the retry layer from `_paged_search` at all.\n  - `_paged_search` (`:315`) — gains `rate_limit_retry: bool = False` and forwards it to\n    `_call_logged`. It **accepts and forwards**; it does NOT pass `True` unconditionally.\n\n**THE OPTED-IN READ CALL SITES — FOUR, exhaustively** (line numbers probed on `main` at\n`529e802da0`, i.e. post-S1):\n  1. `get_issuelinks_map` -> `self._paged_search(...)` at `transport.py:349` — passes `True`.\n  2. `get_comment_map` -> `self._paged_search(...)` at `transport.py:363` — passes `True`.\n  3. `get_comment_map`'s inner PER-ISSUE comment fetch at `transport.py:367` — a direct\n     `_with_connection_retry` call (PATH B above) — passes `True`.\n  4. `get_parent_map` -> `self._paged_search(query, fields=\"parent\")` at `transport.py:461` —\n     passes `True`.\n\n**NOT in scope:** any mutation call site; any change to ADR-0036's connectivity backoff curve; any\nread not in the list of four above (every other read keeps today's behaviour, which is what the\ndefault-OFF flag guarantees).\n\n## Testing\n\n* **the cap-exhaustion contract** — a client that returns 429 forever: assert the escaping\n  exception is `BackendHTTPError` with `code == 429`, AND assert `jira.exceptions.JIRAError` does\n  NOT escape. A test that only checks \"it raised\" passes while the vendor type leaks.\n* **`Retry-After` is honoured when present** — the sleep is driven by the header, not the ADR-0036\n  curve; jitter is applied and the result is CLAMPED to `MAX_BACKOFF_S`.\n* **absent header degrades gracefully** — no header (limiter off / pre-8.6 / Server) behaves exactly\n  as today.\n* **MUTATIONS DO NOT RETRY, enumerated rather than sampled** — assert one 429 fails immediately for\n  EVERY non-opting mutation member of the transport. Enumerate them explicitly; an ellipsis is how\n  a later-added mutation silently inherits the retry.\n* **the opt-in defaults to OFF** — a call that does not pass the flag does not retry a 429.\n* **BOTH PATHS carry the flag** — one test per path, because they fail independently: a\n  `_paged_search`-driven read (PATH A, exercising the `_call_logged` forward) and\n  `get_comment_map`'s per-issue fetch (PATH B, the direct call). A single test over\n  `_paged_search` would pass while the highest-volume read remained unprotected.\n* **`_call_logged` still forwards nothing by default** — its existing callers (every other\n  transport member) must be unaffected, asserted rather than assumed.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [x] [rebar:f2f3-9cb1-335b-4e31] (S1) has LANDED — Gerrit 1179, `main` commit `529e802da0`;\n      `transport.py` 789 -> 683 lines, `retry.py` created at 162 lines\n- [ ] a 429 with a `Retry-After` header is retried on an opted-in READ, with jitter applied and the\n      delay clamped to `MAX_BACKOFF_S`, asserted numerically\n- [ ] `_errors.parse_retry_after` and `_errors.MAX_BACKOFF_S` are REUSED — a test or the diff shows\n      no second parser and no second constant\n- [ ] cap exhaustion raises `BackendHTTPError` with `code == 429`, and a test asserts `JIRAError`\n      does not escape the adapter boundary\n- [ ] EVERY mutation call site still fails on the FIRST 429 — asserted per member over an explicit\n      enumeration of the transport's mutation members, not a sample\n- [ ] the opt-in parameter defaults to OFF on `_with_connection_retry`, on `_call_logged` and on\n      `_paged_search`, asserted, so a future call inherits no retry by omission\n- [ ] `_call_logged` ACCEPTS AND FORWARDS `rate_limit_retry`. This is an EDIT, not a pass-through\n      that already works: today it calls `_with_connection_retry(fn)` with no keyword argument, so\n      without this change the flag is a no-op that still type-checks\n- [ ] `_paged_search` ACCEPTS AND FORWARDS `rate_limit_retry: bool = False` — it does NOT pass\n      `True` unconditionally. The decision, not a requirement to decide: an unconditional `True`\n      would silently grant retry to every FUTURE caller of the helper, the same \"inherits by\n      omission\" hazard the per-call opt-in exists to prevent\n- [ ] ALL FOUR opted-in read call sites listed in Scope pass `True`, asserted PER SITE — including\n      `get_comment_map`'s inner per-issue fetch, which does NOT route through `_paged_search` or\n      `_call_logged` and is the highest-volume read in a pass\n- [ ] the three researched numbers (DC-only, 8.6+, off-by-default; retry-after + up to 20% jitter)\n      are each either confirmed against the target DC version or explicitly marked unverified"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785622341345845001,"uuid":"8f3cb718-0f23-4416-ab87-ef1b145b3497"}